WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:01.420 --> 00:00:07.589
Order of operations is most prevalent in arithmetic,
and, as a result, many logic errors, meaning

00:00:07.589 --> 00:00:12.769
errors in intended calculations, occur with
arithmetic.

00:00:12.769 --> 00:00:16.379
Before we look at a code example, let’s
ensure we understand the order of operations

00:00:16.379 --> 00:00:18.310
related to arithmetic.

00:00:18.310 --> 00:00:20.930
The order is as follows:

00:00:20.930 --> 00:00:21.930
Parentheses

00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:23.310
Exponents

00:00:23.310 --> 00:00:24.310
Multiplication

00:00:24.310 --> 00:00:29.070
Division, including Modulus, which is a remainder
after division

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:30.300
Addition

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:31.949
Subtraction

00:00:31.949 --> 00:00:38.870
A common mnemonic used to remember the order
of operations is Please Excuse My Dear Aunt

00:00:38.870 --> 00:00:47.800
Sally for Parentheses, Exponents, Multiplication,
Division, Addition, and Subtraction.

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:51.960
It is very important to ensure we understand
the order of operations to avoid logic problems

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:54.230
in any calculations within our apps.

00:00:54.230 --> 00:00:58.930
Let’s look at examples of how the order
of operations works with arithmetic.

00:00:58.930 --> 00:01:08.290
Here is a code example, 134-arithmetic, where
the total score should be the base score plus

00:01:08.290 --> 00:01:15.140
the bonus amount, with that total multiplied
by a person’s rank within a game.

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:17.700
That formula is on line 5.

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:26.180
We are looking for a total score of 13,500
on paper, as the base plus the bonus is 4500,

00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:27.490
and the rank is 3.

00:01:27.490 --> 00:01:33.189
But let’s run this code and see what the
result is, as the calculation is currently

00:01:33.189 --> 00:01:34.189
built.

00:01:34.189 --> 00:01:41.150
The result is only 5500, not 13,500.

00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:48.070
In a game situation, a player would not be
happy with a much lower score than what is

00:01:48.070 --> 00:01:49.070
expected.

00:01:49.070 --> 00:01:53.930
Let’s apply the order of operations to the
formula on line 5.

00:01:53.930 --> 00:01:59.789
The perceived problem with this formula is
the assumption that math always occurs left

00:01:59.789 --> 00:02:00.789
to right.

00:02:00.789 --> 00:02:08.850
In this case, one might think that the base
is added to the bonus, totaling 4500, and

00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:15.530
then multiplied by the rank, 3, to get to
13500.

00:02:15.530 --> 00:02:21.910
In arithmetic, the order of operations dictates
that multiplication occurs before addition.

00:02:21.910 --> 00:02:29.690
Thus, the bonus is multiplied by the rank
first, giving us 1500, and then added to the

00:02:29.690 --> 00:02:36.080
base score, 4000, giving us the 5500 result
we see.

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:38.930
How do we fix the formula, then?

00:02:38.930 --> 00:02:45.640
Do you remember what the first priority is
in the order of operations?

00:02:45.640 --> 00:02:49.470
If you said parentheses, you are correct.

00:02:49.470 --> 00:02:54.240
We want the score to be calculated first,
so I will put parentheses around base plus

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:56.370
bonus.

00:02:56.370 --> 00:03:00.959
That part of the score will calculate first
and then multiply by the rank to get the total

00:03:00.959 --> 00:03:01.959
score.

00:03:01.959 --> 00:03:05.340
Let’s rerun the code.

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:07.780
The formula now works as intended.

00:03:07.780 --> 00:03:13.080
A good practice with any formula with multiple
operators is to figure out what must be done

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:21.200
first and put that part of the code in parentheses,
even if the parentheses are unnecessary.

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:26.629
We want to convey to others the top priorities
within a calculation.

00:03:26.629 --> 00:03:32.570
Finally, compared to the other operator types
we have covered so far: assignment, comparison,

00:03:32.570 --> 00:03:35.900
and logical, arithmetic takes priority.

00:03:35.900 --> 00:03:36.920
Why?

00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:41.120
Think of it this way: if we had two calculations
to compare to see if one is greater than the

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:46.010
other, wouldn’t we need to do the calculations
first?

00:03:46.010 --> 00:03:50.330
We would, and that’s why arithmetic is the
top priority among the different types of

00:03:50.330 --> 00:03:54.750
operators used in Python and other programming
languages.

